## Undeclared Colors: Galaxy RG Corp's Rainbow of Errors  
If you're a fan of colorful desserts, you might want to double-check the ingredient labels--or lack thereof. Galaxy RG Corp is at the center of this week's recalls, with a whopping nine products pulled due to undeclared artificial colors. From Marquesita de Mango to Cheesecake de Fresa, the common denominators are Yellow #5, Yellow #6, and Red #40--dyes that are FDA-approved but must be disclosed to inform consumers with sensitivities. The lapse in transparency here is cause for concern and serves as a reminder to read labels carefully--even on your favorite treats.  
  
## Supplements with a Side of Salmonella  
Total Nutrition Inc's "green superfood" supplements made headlines this week with a Class I recall. The cause? Potential Salmonella contamination--one of the most serious foodborne pathogens. While the name suggests health benefits, the inclusion of this opportunistic bacterium is hardly what one expects when reaching for a bottle of Moringa capsules.  
  
## Recalls by the Numbers  
Year-to-date, the FDA has issued 577 recalls, breaking them into Class I (176), Class II (361), and Class III (39). Compared to last year's monthly average of 80 recalls, June 2026 saw a slightly lower total of 78. However, the uptick in allergen-related recalls this year--283 so far--is a stark reminder of the importance of accurate labeling.  
  
## Repeat Offenders: Should They Stay in Business?  
With 87 repeat offenders this year alone, it's worth questioning the accountability of manufacturers. Among them, Moonlight Packing Corporation, Galaxy RG Corp, and Albertsons Companies LLC continue to make appearances on the FDA's recall list. Are we prioritizing profit over public health? Or is the system in need of stricter penalties for repeat offenses? These are questions we must all grapple with--the cost of cutting corners is often paid in the currency of consumer trust.  
  
## Concluding Thoughts  
This week's recalls--riddled with undeclared allergens and contaminants--underscore the critical need for transparency and rigorous safety protocols in food production. Whether you're a cautious label-reader or a carefree snacker, staying informed can be the best way to protect yourself and your loved ones.
